  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nEarly this morning, our primary weather concerns are widespread\nstratus, patchy fog, and light rain. The light rain, affecting \nmainly the southern portions of our forecast area, is being \ngenerated by a band of frontogenesis generated by a surface low \nover the the IA/NE border, out in advance of another over \neastern Colorado. At the upper levels, an upper low moving \nacross the Rockies is pushing shortwaves out across the plains \nahead of it. This system is expected to continue to push east \nthrough the weekend. The stratus deck has expanded significantly\novernight, causing earlier fog to dissipate in many areas. \nHowever, where skies briefly cleared, patchy fog remains a \nfactor early this morning. With persistent east to northeast \nflow as the surface low remains to our south, expect mostly \ncloudy skies to dominate the day. This cloud cover will keep \ntemperatures on the chilly side, particularly in northwest \nWisconsin, where highs will only reach the upper 50s to low 60s.\nNorth-central Minnesota may see some breaks in the clouds later\ntoday, allowing temperatures to warm into the mid and upper \n60s.\n\nMoving into tonight and Sunday, the aforementioned low-pressure\nsystem will push further east, taking the light rain chances \nwith it. However, high pressure will push in from the north, \ncausing the pressure gradient to tighten, leading to gusty \nnortheast winds, particularly over the western arm of Lake \nSuperior. This will usher in a cooler and drier airmass. The \npersistent northeast flow on Sunday will keep conditions brisk \nand cool, especially near the lake. This will also cause clouds \nto persist, though there may be enough gaps for some peeks at \nsunshine.\n\nBeginning Monday and continuing through the upcoming week, high\npressure will firmly establish itself over the Upper Midwest \nand Great Lakes region. This will lead to a prolonged period of \nquiet, dry weather. The main story will be the cool overnight \ntemperatures under clear skies and light winds, which may cause \nfrost development each night from Sunday night through Wednesday\nnight, especially for inland areas of northern Minnesota and \nnorthwest Wisconsin. High temperatures will gradually moderate \nas the week progresses, returning to near-normal values in the \nmid-to-upper 60s by late in the week.",
